1
00:00:00,960 --> 00:00:03,960
It can be enjoyed as a solid or as a liquid.

2
00:00:03,961 --> 00:00:07,360
It can be served hot, cold, room temperature, or frozen.

3
00:00:07,361 --> 00:00:09,480
It's an essential part in religious rites,

4
00:00:09,481 --> 00:00:12,000
holiday traditions, even romantic gestures.

5
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,200
And when placed into a box, it can serve as a metaphor

6
00:00:16,201 --> 00:00:19,000
for the uncertainty of life itself.

7
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:22,360
But today, unlike life itself, I know exactly

8
00:00:22,361 --> 00:00:24,320
what I'm going to get.

9
00:00:24,321 --> 00:00:25,520
Chocolate.

10
00:00:25,521 --> 00:00:28,000
Even though the United States has less than 5%

11
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:31,900
to the world's population, it consumes close to 20%

12
00:00:31,901 --> 00:00:33,440
of the world's chocolate.

13
00:00:33,441 --> 00:00:38,640
The United States eats almost 100 pounds of chocolate

14
00:00:38,641 --> 00:00:41,520
every second of every day.

15
00:00:41,521 --> 00:00:46,080
And chocolate generates $100 billion

16
00:00:46,081 --> 00:00:48,960
in worldwide revenues per year.

17
00:00:48,961 --> 00:00:52,060
Whether it comes in a truffle, a cup of hot cocoa,

18
00:00:52,061 --> 00:00:55,220
or a scoop of ice cream, nothing compares

19
00:00:55,221 --> 00:00:57,760
to the decadent indulgence of chocolate.

20
00:00:57,761 --> 00:01:01,760
And today, we're doing a deep dive on chocolate candy.

21
00:01:01,761 --> 00:01:04,160
Where does the word chocolate come from?

22
00:01:04,161 --> 00:01:05,960
What are some of the bizarre foods

23
00:01:05,961 --> 00:01:08,360
that people coat in chocolate?

24
00:01:08,361 --> 00:01:11,460
And how did Milton Hershey revolutionize

25
00:01:11,461 --> 00:01:13,920
the way we enjoy this beloved sweet treat?

26
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:17,200
How does this chocolate economy work?

27
00:01:17,201 --> 00:01:19,620
How does a tropical fruit grown around the equator

28
00:01:19,621 --> 00:01:21,780
become one of the world's most beloved sweets?

29
00:01:21,781 --> 00:01:25,380
And how many tiny cocoa beans does it take

30
00:01:25,381 --> 00:01:28,220
to keep up with this gigantic demand?

31
00:01:28,221 --> 00:01:30,380
There's only one way to find out.

32
00:01:30,381 --> 00:01:33,380
This is Modern Marvel's Chocolate.

33
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:51,980
Today, I'm in the city of brotherly love,

34
00:01:51,981 --> 00:01:55,140
Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, the US's first

35
00:01:55,141 --> 00:01:57,940
home to Independence Hall and the Liberty Bell.

36
00:01:57,980 --> 00:02:00,940
For a while, Philadelphia was one of the most active ports

37
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:01,980
in the U.S.

38
00:02:02,020 --> 00:02:04,420
Because of that, most of the cacao beans entering

39
00:02:04,440 --> 00:02:06,920
the United States during the 19th century

40
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:08,680
were processed right here.

41
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:11,860
About 100 years before that, in 1739,

42
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:15,000
Benjamin Franklin was selling chocolate out of his print shop

43
00:02:15,020 --> 00:02:17,960
just a stone's throw from where I'm standing on Market Street.

44
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,740
Today, Shane Confectionery remains the oldest

45
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:24,240
continuously operating candy store

46
00:02:24,241 --> 00:02:25,740
in the entire United States.

47
00:02:25,741 --> 00:02:30,480
It started right here in this very building in 1863.

48
00:02:30,481 --> 00:02:32,120
For perspective, that's the same year

49
00:02:32,121 --> 00:02:36,080
that Abraham Lincoln issued the Emancipation Proclamation.

50
00:02:36,081 --> 00:02:39,080
I can't wait to check this place out.

51
00:02:39,081 --> 00:02:43,500
The magic of Shane hits me the moment I set foot inside.

52
00:02:43,501 --> 00:02:48,000
I'm immediately struck by the sweet smell of enticing treats.

53
00:02:48,001 --> 00:02:50,400
This chocolatey scent has been permeating

54
00:02:50,401 --> 00:02:54,240
every nook and cranny of this shop since the Civil War.

55
00:02:54,241 --> 00:02:57,440
In fact, this place is so old,

56
00:02:57,540 --> 00:03:00,440
the cash register hadn't even been invented

57
00:03:00,540 --> 00:03:04,280
until long after the first customers were served.

58
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:06,580
Today, we're going to be learning

59
00:03:06,680 --> 00:03:09,380
some delicious chocolate secrets.

60
00:03:09,480 --> 00:03:12,200
I feel like I've been handed a golden ticket.

61
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:15,000
And the wizard behind it all is head chocolate maker,

62
00:03:15,100 --> 00:03:16,200
Kevin Pascoe.

63
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:18,200
This building and this block have a ton

64
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:20,000
of really, really rich confectionery history.

65
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:21,600
I mean, Philadelphia as a whole does.

66
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:23,840
Part of the mission of this establishment

67
00:03:23,841 --> 00:03:25,600
is to kind of highlight that history.

68
00:03:25,601 --> 00:03:27,640
Philadelphia's chocolate connection

69
00:03:27,641 --> 00:03:29,920
reaches back to the 18th century,

70
00:03:29,921 --> 00:03:33,040
when chocolate was touted by elite citizens

71
00:03:33,041 --> 00:03:36,680
like Dr. Benjamin Rush, a physician and politician

72
00:03:36,681 --> 00:03:39,280
who signed the Declaration of Independence.

73
00:03:39,281 --> 00:03:43,120
Rush promoted drinking chocolate not just as a treat,

74
00:03:43,121 --> 00:03:45,920
but as treatment for smallpox.

75
00:03:45,921 --> 00:03:49,040
The current owners got here not all that long ago,

76
00:03:49,041 --> 00:03:51,360
and we only started making bean-to-bar chocolate

77
00:03:51,361 --> 00:03:53,640
about six years ago here in this space.

78
00:03:53,641 --> 00:03:58,640
Making bean-to-bar chocolate means that Shane takes raw cocoa beans

79
00:03:58,680 --> 00:04:03,280
and handcrafts them into their prized small batch chocolate.

80
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:05,520
So these beans come to us from Colombia,

81
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:06,920
from a region called Tumaco,

82
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:08,120
and it works really, really well

83
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:10,360
because it's got a big, fat chocolate backbone.

84
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:11,800
The batch that we're gonna do today

85
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:13,200
is gonna be about 100 pounds.

86
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:15,760
The way that we sort them is literally just by hand.

87
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:18,760
I'm gonna spread out a scoopful of beans onto this grate here.

88
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:21,800
That must be so, like, time-consuming for you guys.

89
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:23,640
I can do it pretty quickly.

90
00:04:23,641 --> 00:04:26,040
Now you're just bragging.

91
00:04:26,041 --> 00:04:28,720
Big brand chocolate companies have machines

92
00:04:28,721 --> 00:04:32,620
that can sort hundreds of pounds of beans in minutes.

93
00:04:32,621 --> 00:04:35,580
But Shane prefers the hands-on approach.

94
00:04:35,581 --> 00:04:38,020
Sorting by hand really gives us a little bit of a connection

95
00:04:38,021 --> 00:04:39,460
to what we're doing in a way.

96
00:04:39,461 --> 00:04:41,660
To really start at the beginning with the raw cocoa beans,

97
00:04:41,661 --> 00:04:42,960
it gives people sort of a window

98
00:04:42,961 --> 00:04:44,800
into the building blocks of chocolate.

99
00:04:44,801 --> 00:04:48,040
So you're looking for beans that are really, really tiny,

100
00:04:48,041 --> 00:04:50,040
beans that are flat.

101
00:04:50,041 --> 00:04:52,700
I expected these to smell like chocolate,

102
00:04:52,701 --> 00:04:56,300
you know, that fudgy, cocoa-y smell.

103
00:04:56,301 --> 00:04:59,580
But it actually, it has a stringency that's similar to soap

104
00:04:59,581 --> 00:05:03,340
or like a cleaning product or vinegar kind of thing.

105
00:05:03,341 --> 00:05:04,620
Is that okay?

106
00:05:04,621 --> 00:05:05,980
Is there something wrong with these beans,

107
00:05:05,981 --> 00:05:07,140
or is that expected?

108
00:05:07,141 --> 00:05:08,820
No, I'd say that's pretty normal.

109
00:05:08,821 --> 00:05:10,780
And that's one of the things that's sort of a byproduct

110
00:05:10,781 --> 00:05:11,780
of fermentation.

111
00:05:11,781 --> 00:05:14,220
These beans are fermented before they come to us,

112
00:05:14,221 --> 00:05:16,660
and that fermentation imparts a good amount of the aroma

113
00:05:16,661 --> 00:05:18,500
that we smell pre-roast.

114
00:05:18,501 --> 00:05:21,800
Pre-roast and even before getting to Shane,

115
00:05:21,801 --> 00:05:25,340
cocoa beans have to be grown, then fermented.

116
00:05:25,341 --> 00:05:29,680
These treasured beans grow inside the pods of the cacao tree.

117
00:05:29,681 --> 00:05:33,420
Cacao trees are native to Central and South America,

118
00:05:33,421 --> 00:05:36,940
and the earliest known use of cacao started there.

119
00:05:36,941 --> 00:05:40,680
3,000 years ago, people in modern-day Honduras

120
00:05:40,681 --> 00:05:42,720
were fermenting cacao.

121
00:05:42,721 --> 00:05:45,320
They were likely trying to make booze,

122
00:05:45,321 --> 00:05:49,560
but stumbled onto something that to some is even better,

123
00:05:49,561 --> 00:05:50,660
chocolate.

124
00:05:50,661 --> 00:05:53,700
Today, we still grow and ferment cacao,

125
00:05:53,701 --> 00:05:58,200
but only in areas 20 degrees north and south of the equator.

126
00:05:58,201 --> 00:06:01,200
This section is called the cacao belt.

127
00:06:01,201 --> 00:06:05,100
On the upper end of this belt is a rare chocolate farm

128
00:06:05,101 --> 00:06:06,800
located in the US.

129
00:06:06,801 --> 00:06:08,580
Aloha, my name is Koa.

130
00:06:08,581 --> 00:06:11,640
We're on the lovely island of Kauai in Hawaii.

131
00:06:11,641 --> 00:06:14,080
On a pair of five-acre farms,

132
00:06:14,081 --> 00:06:18,420
Koa Kahili grows some of the most well-known types of cacao.

133
00:06:18,421 --> 00:06:20,420
There are two large families of cacao.

134
00:06:20,421 --> 00:06:23,860
They're called criollo and forestero.

135
00:06:23,861 --> 00:06:28,160
Forestero accounts for about 95% of global production.

136
00:06:28,161 --> 00:06:32,040
Like all cacao, both forestero and criollo

137
00:06:32,041 --> 00:06:33,900
are grown in the cacao belt.

138
00:06:33,901 --> 00:06:36,760
But if forestero is a really great burger,

139
00:06:36,761 --> 00:06:40,700
then criollo is more like a filet mignon, more rich, more

140
00:06:40,701 --> 00:06:43,040
rare, and more prized for its flavor.

141
00:06:43,041 --> 00:06:46,040
Criollo beans are very, very rare.

142
00:06:46,041 --> 00:06:50,420
And the reason is that they are very susceptible to diseases.

143
00:06:50,421 --> 00:06:51,760
And to pests?

144
00:06:51,761 --> 00:06:55,660
Despite their differences, all cacao trees bear pods.

145
00:06:55,661 --> 00:06:59,900
And inside these pods are kernels of a sweet white fruit,

146
00:06:59,901 --> 00:07:01,740
which cover the cocoa beans.

147
00:07:01,741 --> 00:07:03,140
So we're going to have to see which ones are ripe.

148
00:07:03,141 --> 00:07:05,000
So you did a quick scrape test.

149
00:07:05,001 --> 00:07:06,780
It doesn't hurt the pod because it's really thick.

150
00:07:06,781 --> 00:07:08,340
If it's green, it's not ripe.

151
00:07:10,480 --> 00:07:13,880
If it's yellow or an orange, it's ripe.

152
00:07:16,940 --> 00:07:19,540
With a fresh haul of harvested pods in hand,

153
00:07:19,541 --> 00:07:22,640
The next step will be to crack them open

154
00:07:22,641 --> 00:07:25,180
and remove the beans for fermentation.

155
00:07:25,181 --> 00:07:28,720
We're going to take our wet beans and put them in the box.

156
00:07:28,721 --> 00:07:32,560
The beans then get fermented in mahogany crates

157
00:07:32,561 --> 00:07:34,420
packed with banana leaves.

158
00:07:34,421 --> 00:07:37,260
Fermentation changes the flavor of the bean

159
00:07:37,261 --> 00:07:40,020
from their natural bitterness to something closer

160
00:07:40,021 --> 00:07:42,560
to the chocolate that we all know and love.

161
00:07:42,561 --> 00:07:46,060
On the underside of the leaf, there's this natural yeast.

162
00:07:46,061 --> 00:07:49,400
As the yeast chomps away at the sugars of the white fruit,

163
00:07:49,401 --> 00:07:53,500
it breaks down, leaving behind a brown bean.

164
00:07:53,600 --> 00:07:56,840
It takes about a week to ferment.

165
00:07:56,940 --> 00:08:00,580
And a week later, we're ready for the final step,

166
00:08:00,680 --> 00:08:02,140
drying the beans.

167
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:04,980
You want to sun dry them for about three weeks

168
00:08:05,080 --> 00:08:07,480
until the moisture content is about 5%.

169
00:08:07,580 --> 00:08:12,120
You can take one in your fingers and crack it like that.

170
00:08:12,220 --> 00:08:14,500
At most farms, dried beans are then

171
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:17,300
packed and sent to chocolate makers like Shane,

172
00:08:17,301 --> 00:08:20,800
where they're sorted and ready to get roasted.

173
00:08:20,801 --> 00:08:22,880
What we're going to do here is we're going to load these

174
00:08:22,881 --> 00:08:26,040
onto these perforated trays.

175
00:08:26,041 --> 00:08:28,080
And some folks roast in like a big drum roaster,

176
00:08:28,081 --> 00:08:30,780
kind of like the way you'd see for coffee.

177
00:08:30,781 --> 00:08:32,880
But here, we try to economize on space

178
00:08:32,881 --> 00:08:34,380
by roasting in a convection oven.

179
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:40,660
So talk to me a little bit just about roasting.

180
00:08:40,661 --> 00:08:42,700
How does roasting affect this bean?

181
00:08:42,701 --> 00:08:44,320
Because essentially, that's what we're dealing with, right?

182
00:08:44,321 --> 00:08:45,320
May I touch them again?

183
00:08:45,321 --> 00:08:46,320
Of course.

184
00:08:46,321 --> 00:08:50,120
You can really express, like, the best qualities of a given cocoa

185
00:08:50,121 --> 00:08:51,920
through, like, a great roast, and you could take

186
00:08:51,921 --> 00:08:53,920
what would otherwise be a really delicious bean,

187
00:08:53,921 --> 00:08:55,800
and you can kind of kill its flavor with a bad roast.

188
00:08:55,801 --> 00:08:57,840
And we're still learning about new varietals.

189
00:08:57,841 --> 00:09:00,200
It's an agricultural product, and it grows out of the ground,

190
00:09:00,201 --> 00:09:01,200
and as such...

191
00:09:01,201 --> 00:09:02,200
There's lots of variations.

192
00:09:02,201 --> 00:09:03,400
There's natural differentiation.

193
00:09:03,401 --> 00:09:05,680
Soil conditions, rainfall, things like that,

194
00:09:05,681 --> 00:09:06,880
they might taste a little different,

195
00:09:06,881 --> 00:09:09,680
so we have to adjust our roasting to compensate for that.

196
00:09:09,681 --> 00:09:14,720
You're using terms that are very much the same terms used with wine

197
00:09:14,721 --> 00:09:17,120
when you're talking about varietals and rainfall,

198
00:09:17,121 --> 00:09:19,020
soil composition itself.

199
00:09:19,021 --> 00:09:20,960
So I think it's really kind of cool.

200
00:09:20,961 --> 00:09:23,020
The whole roasting process, time-wise,

201
00:09:23,021 --> 00:09:24,960
start to finish, is how long?

202
00:09:24,961 --> 00:09:26,860
For this bean, about 40 minutes.

203
00:09:26,861 --> 00:09:29,360
Roasting is the first step in bringing out

204
00:09:29,361 --> 00:09:33,180
real chocolate flavor, but it's far from the last.

205
00:09:33,181 --> 00:09:36,280
After the roast, the beans are cracked open

206
00:09:36,281 --> 00:09:39,080
to separate the paper-like outer shell

207
00:09:39,081 --> 00:09:41,180
from the chocolatey inner nibs.

208
00:09:41,181 --> 00:09:44,180
The nibs get fed into a grinder

209
00:09:44,181 --> 00:09:47,880
where they're ground into this shiny, fudgy goo.

210
00:09:47,980 --> 00:09:51,880
That fudgy liquid gets mixed with sugar and cocoa butter

211
00:09:51,980 --> 00:09:55,060
to sweeten and fatten it for its transformation

212
00:09:55,160 --> 00:09:56,600
into chocolate candy.

213
00:09:56,700 --> 00:10:00,760
The sweetened, molten chocolate is set into solid blocks

214
00:10:00,860 --> 00:10:04,000
that Shane ages for two whole months.

215
00:10:04,100 --> 00:10:06,940
Finally, the aged chocolate is melted down

216
00:10:07,040 --> 00:10:10,140
and cooled repeatedly, because this step ensures

217
00:10:10,240 --> 00:10:13,140
that once it's molded, it's a shiny bar

218
00:10:13,141 --> 00:10:15,140
of chocolatey goodness.

219
00:10:15,180 --> 00:10:17,140
And the first hint of that magical bar

220
00:10:17,180 --> 00:10:19,820
at the end of the road starts right here

221
00:10:19,840 --> 00:10:22,380
with our freshly roasted beans.

222
00:10:22,420 --> 00:10:24,660
I know these are hot, but may I try one of these?

223
00:10:24,680 --> 00:10:26,460
Yeah, absolutely. Just watch you don't burn yourself.

224
00:10:26,480 --> 00:10:27,360
All right.

225
00:10:27,380 --> 00:10:28,860
And we're just gonna kind of, in your hand,

226
00:10:28,880 --> 00:10:30,120
you can just kind of crack it up.

227
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:32,260
You can kind of peel that outer husk off.

228
00:10:32,300 --> 00:10:34,060
But that's the actual cocoa bean right here.

229
00:10:34,100 --> 00:10:35,160
And you're left, exactly.

230
00:10:35,200 --> 00:10:36,400
You're left with the inner nib.

231
00:10:36,420 --> 00:10:39,160
So this is all the husk, and this is bean.

232
00:10:39,161 --> 00:10:42,700
It's crazy.

233
00:10:42,840 --> 00:10:45,800
There's, like, really none of that vinegary smell

234
00:10:45,801 --> 00:10:46,840
remaining at all.

235
00:10:49,300 --> 00:10:50,000
Wow.

236
00:10:51,940 --> 00:10:54,640
The history of chocolate candy is littered

237
00:10:54,641 --> 00:10:57,860
with iconic brand names, like the Baby Ruth Bar.

238
00:10:58,360 --> 00:11:02,260
This block of milk chocolate, nougat, caramel, and peanuts

239
00:11:02,261 --> 00:11:04,980
was originally called the candy cake,

240
00:11:05,300 --> 00:11:07,920
but was renamed in 1921,

241
00:11:07,921 --> 00:11:11,920
The same year a certain New York Yankee was at the height of his fame.

242
00:11:11,921 --> 00:11:16,920
This sweet treat also happens to be a childhood favorite of a certain Yankee fan.

243
00:11:16,921 --> 00:11:18,920
Yours truly.

244
00:11:18,921 --> 00:11:21,920
In my experience, a baby Ruth can help fix everything,

245
00:11:21,921 --> 00:11:24,920
from a skinned knee to a broken heart.

246
00:11:24,921 --> 00:11:28,920
Just the thought of a baby Ruth has me feeling like a kid in a candy store,

247
00:11:28,921 --> 00:11:31,920
which is exactly where I am.

248
00:11:31,921 --> 00:11:35,920
At Shane, I'm getting my first taste of chocolate delight.

249
00:11:35,921 --> 00:11:37,160
I mean, I try one of these.

250
00:11:37,161 --> 00:11:37,980
Absolutely.

251
00:11:37,981 --> 00:11:38,820
Just watch it.

252
00:11:38,821 --> 00:11:39,820
Don't burn yourself.

253
00:11:45,520 --> 00:11:46,420
Mmm.

254
00:11:50,360 --> 00:11:52,100
A little floral.

255
00:11:52,101 --> 00:11:54,980
The flavor is a little bit like that corner brownie

256
00:11:54,981 --> 00:11:56,340
where it touches the pan.

257
00:11:56,341 --> 00:11:57,480
Right, right.

258
00:11:57,481 --> 00:12:00,080
So it's that fudge, but more of a char to it.

259
00:12:00,081 --> 00:12:01,380
And it has a little bit of roast.

260
00:12:01,381 --> 00:12:02,680
Yeah.

261
00:12:02,681 --> 00:12:03,820
Yeah, that roast, and there's a little bit of like a chicory

262
00:12:03,821 --> 00:12:06,320
like a campfire coffee note.

263
00:12:06,321 --> 00:12:10,000
It's remarkable that just time and temperature

264
00:12:10,001 --> 00:12:12,000
have taken this organic thing

265
00:12:12,001 --> 00:12:14,160
and brought out whole new characteristics of it.

266
00:12:14,161 --> 00:12:16,000
Wow.

267
00:12:16,001 --> 00:12:18,660
While we've unlocked new flavors in this chocolate,

268
00:12:18,661 --> 00:12:22,500
it turns out we're just scratching the surface.

269
00:12:22,501 --> 00:12:24,840
Chocolate is a really, really complex food.

270
00:12:24,841 --> 00:12:26,880
There are hundreds of chemical compounds in chocolate

271
00:12:26,881 --> 00:12:28,680
that give it a lot of complexity.

272
00:12:28,681 --> 00:12:32,240
In fact, over 600 flavor compounds

273
00:12:32,241 --> 00:12:34,600
compounds have been found in chocolate,

274
00:12:34,601 --> 00:12:38,120
making it one of the most complex foods on Earth.

275
00:12:38,121 --> 00:12:40,380
And it is the complexity of chocolate flavor

276
00:12:40,381 --> 00:12:43,080
that lends itself to a lot of different uses

277
00:12:43,081 --> 00:12:45,420
beyond simple chocolate bars.

278
00:12:45,421 --> 00:12:47,880
Some of these uses include using chocolate

279
00:12:47,881 --> 00:12:51,760
to coat things that are a little unconventional.

280
00:12:51,761 --> 00:12:54,720
We normally think of it as something only in dessert,

281
00:12:54,721 --> 00:12:57,200
something that's sweet, but it has a huge applications

282
00:12:57,201 --> 00:12:58,040
and savories.

283
00:12:58,041 --> 00:13:02,240
Like bacon, potato chips,

284
00:13:02,241 --> 00:13:03,580
and popcorn.

285
00:13:03,581 --> 00:13:06,480
Chocolate can go with so many different things.

286
00:13:06,481 --> 00:13:10,340
It really can coat porcini mushrooms and shrimp

287
00:13:10,341 --> 00:13:14,460
and all sorts of delicious and bizarre things.

288
00:13:14,461 --> 00:13:17,680
And when you come into other cultures, all bets are off.

289
00:13:17,681 --> 00:13:20,520
There's several makers in Brazil

290
00:13:20,521 --> 00:13:22,400
and all over South America, really,

291
00:13:22,401 --> 00:13:26,600
who are making things with worms and ants and crickets.

292
00:13:26,601 --> 00:13:29,700
You're going to find scorpions, grasshoppers,

293
00:13:29,701 --> 00:13:31,740
really common in Mexico for many, many years.

294
00:13:31,741 --> 00:13:33,500
so insects and chocolate are huge.

295
00:13:33,501 --> 00:13:37,420
That lends itself to, you know, what can't you cover it with?

296
00:13:37,421 --> 00:13:39,880
The only thing that gets in your way

297
00:13:39,881 --> 00:13:43,220
when you think about what chocolate might cover

298
00:13:43,221 --> 00:13:45,680
is how squeamish you are.

299
00:13:45,681 --> 00:13:50,260
Over at Shane, the next step is to crack these beans open.

300
00:13:50,261 --> 00:13:52,560
Now that our roasted beans are nice and cool,

301
00:13:52,561 --> 00:13:57,140
I'm going to dump them on into the winnower just like this.

302
00:13:57,141 --> 00:13:58,060
Shall I do the honors?

303
00:13:58,061 --> 00:14:00,000
Oh, please do, because I will drop them everywhere.

304
00:14:00,001 --> 00:14:02,900
To get at the nibs inside our entire batch,

305
00:14:02,901 --> 00:14:05,600
we'll use a special machine known as the winnower.

306
00:14:05,601 --> 00:14:09,820
All right, let's get to winnowing.

307
00:14:09,821 --> 00:14:12,080
Once Kevin loads the beans into the hopper

308
00:14:12,081 --> 00:14:15,380
and releases a catch, the winnower gets to work.

309
00:14:17,820 --> 00:14:20,260
The beans first fall into the cracker,

310
00:14:20,261 --> 00:14:22,060
which has a blade that will chop them up

311
00:14:22,061 --> 00:14:25,960
into a mix of husks and dark brown nibs.

312
00:14:25,961 --> 00:14:28,700
The crushed beans then tumble down a tube

313
00:14:28,701 --> 00:14:31,740
where precise vacuum pressure will suck up all the papery

314
00:14:31,741 --> 00:14:36,100
husks, leaving behind the solid nibs.

315
00:14:36,101 --> 00:14:39,740
Because the husk is so much lighter than the nib,

316
00:14:39,741 --> 00:14:42,220
it just automatically separates it.

317
00:14:42,221 --> 00:14:43,480
Exactly, yeah.

318
00:14:43,481 --> 00:14:45,640
The heavier nibs will fall down these tubes

319
00:14:45,641 --> 00:14:46,880
and into the bucket.

320
00:14:46,881 --> 00:14:49,320
And we want the lighter husks to be sucked back up

321
00:14:49,321 --> 00:14:52,480
and into the vacuum chamber.

322
00:14:52,481 --> 00:14:53,420
Shall we fire it up?

323
00:14:53,421 --> 00:14:56,220
Fire it up.

324
00:14:56,221 --> 00:14:58,320
Like much of the machinery at Shane,

325
00:14:58,321 --> 00:15:02,400
The winnower here is designed to handle small batch chocolate

326
00:15:02,401 --> 00:15:03,101
making.

327
00:15:06,500 --> 00:15:09,560
But some artisan chocolate makers go big,

328
00:15:09,561 --> 00:15:12,260
like Theo Chocolate in Seattle.

329
00:15:12,261 --> 00:15:14,400
In the 15 years since their start,

330
00:15:14,401 --> 00:15:18,440
Theo has raked in over a dozen national and international

331
00:15:18,441 --> 00:15:20,200
awards for their chocolate making.

332
00:15:20,201 --> 00:15:22,420
And they've gone from hand delivering their chocolates

333
00:15:22,421 --> 00:15:25,820
locally to booming sales that have taken their products

334
00:15:25,821 --> 00:15:27,020
coast to coast.

335
00:15:27,021 --> 00:15:29,720
Now, they're one of the most highly regarded chocolate

336
00:15:29,721 --> 00:15:31,120
makers in the US.

337
00:15:31,121 --> 00:15:35,320
And here, at their 30,000 square foot chocolate factory,

338
00:15:35,321 --> 00:15:38,100
they deal with sacks of beans so large,

339
00:15:38,101 --> 00:15:42,100
they need industrial lifts to move them to the roaster.

340
00:15:42,101 --> 00:15:44,900
Each one of these sacks can weigh up to 1,000 pounds.

341
00:15:47,680 --> 00:15:50,020
And this is where they cook.

342
00:15:50,021 --> 00:15:52,620
It's also the reason that if you come to Seattle and visit us,

343
00:15:52,621 --> 00:15:55,220
there's like a four block radius around the building

344
00:15:55,221 --> 00:15:57,020
where it smells like brownies.

345
00:15:57,021 --> 00:15:59,820
And once the beans are done roasting...

346
00:15:59,821 --> 00:16:00,820
There we go.

347
00:16:00,821 --> 00:16:02,520
...they're released into a tank

348
00:16:02,521 --> 00:16:04,620
where they're rotated and cooled.

349
00:16:04,621 --> 00:16:07,940
This is what 600 pounds of cacao beans look like.

350
00:16:07,941 --> 00:16:09,660
There's a little hole in the bottom of this tank.

351
00:16:09,661 --> 00:16:11,100
The beans go through the hole

352
00:16:11,101 --> 00:16:13,200
up into this machine, the winnower.

353
00:16:13,201 --> 00:16:17,380
This towering structure here is Theo's winnower.

354
00:16:17,381 --> 00:16:19,680
The beans enter through that top compartment.

355
00:16:19,681 --> 00:16:22,180
They get hit by a steel plate, so they shatter,

356
00:16:22,181 --> 00:16:24,280
and then move down this staircase.

357
00:16:24,281 --> 00:16:26,280
As each nib hits each level,

358
00:16:26,281 --> 00:16:28,320
These silver boxes are vacuums,

359
00:16:28,321 --> 00:16:30,580
and those vacuums are sucking up the shell.

360
00:16:30,581 --> 00:16:33,520
The high-tech machinery at this Washington factory

361
00:16:33,521 --> 00:16:38,520
helps Theo pump out 70,000 pounds of chocolate every week.

362
00:16:38,521 --> 00:16:42,020
But human ingenuity is also at play.

363
00:16:42,021 --> 00:16:44,300
At the end of the day, someone is here checking the temperature.

364
00:16:44,301 --> 00:16:46,100
Someone is here tasting the nibs

365
00:16:46,101 --> 00:16:48,240
and seeing if we need to modify anything.

366
00:16:48,241 --> 00:16:50,140
So even though a machine's helping us out,

367
00:16:50,141 --> 00:16:52,440
it's still reliant upon, you know, a human there

368
00:16:52,441 --> 00:16:54,200
to make sure we can make the right product.

369
00:16:54,201 --> 00:16:56,840
And this human touch is equally essential

370
00:16:56,841 --> 00:17:00,140
at small batch shops like Shane.

371
00:17:00,141 --> 00:17:03,940
The nibs here might not weigh in at 600 pounds a batch.

372
00:17:03,941 --> 00:17:06,640
Yeah, so that all looks pretty clean to me.

373
00:17:06,641 --> 00:17:10,780
But it's the taste that counts.

374
00:17:10,781 --> 00:17:14,680
So these right here, the meat of the bean, as it were,

375
00:17:14,681 --> 00:17:16,520
is just pure cacao nib.

376
00:17:24,200 --> 00:17:25,200
It's funny.

377
00:17:25,201 --> 00:17:30,040
So I was looking for, like, a very chocolatey taste.

378
00:17:30,041 --> 00:17:33,700
But it's much more like a roasted flavor.

379
00:17:33,701 --> 00:17:36,040
But then there's, like, that back note of, like,

380
00:17:36,041 --> 00:17:40,440
a dark chocolate and almost like red fruit,

381
00:17:40,441 --> 00:17:44,080
like a dark cherry, blackberry kind of note to it.

382
00:17:44,081 --> 00:17:45,160
And it's really amazing.

383
00:17:45,161 --> 00:17:46,580
You'll see, like, what refining does.

384
00:17:46,581 --> 00:17:49,360
It really will pull that chocolate note out

385
00:17:49,361 --> 00:17:52,820
and kind of, like, mellow out some of those other higher notes.

386
00:17:52,821 --> 00:17:54,820
Now that we've finished winnowing,

387
00:17:54,821 --> 00:17:56,820
these are pretty much ready to start refining.

388
00:17:56,821 --> 00:17:58,820
Refining the nibs means throwing them

389
00:17:58,821 --> 00:18:00,820
into a machine called the melanger

390
00:18:00,821 --> 00:18:03,820
and crushing them into a chocolatey goo.

391
00:18:03,821 --> 00:18:06,820
Mm, just saying that makes my mouth water.

392
00:18:06,821 --> 00:18:08,820
All right, so the melanger's the next step?

393
00:18:08,821 --> 00:18:10,820
That's right, yeah, so let's head on over there.

394
00:18:10,821 --> 00:18:12,820
Show me the way, brother. All right.

395
00:18:12,821 --> 00:18:14,820
How many Hershey's milk chocolate bars

396
00:18:14,821 --> 00:18:18,820
can be produced from one cacao tree every year?

397
00:18:18,821 --> 00:18:21,820
Is it 10, 100,

398
00:18:21,821 --> 00:18:23,820
or 1,000.

399
00:18:26,820 --> 00:18:30,820
Every year, a single cacao tree only produces enough beans

400
00:18:30,821 --> 00:18:34,820
to make 10 regular-sized Hershey Milk chocolate bars.

401
00:18:37,820 --> 00:18:40,820
At the oldest candy shop in the US,

402
00:18:40,821 --> 00:18:43,820
chocolate-making can be a real grind.

403
00:18:43,821 --> 00:18:47,820
It usually takes 24 hours to crush cocoa nibs

404
00:18:47,821 --> 00:18:50,820
into this vat of chocolatey liquid.

405
00:18:50,821 --> 00:18:54,320
But lucky for us, Kevin's got a batch from yesterday

406
00:18:54,321 --> 00:18:56,320
ready for our tasting pleasure.

407
00:18:56,321 --> 00:18:58,160
All right, this is remarkable.

408
00:18:58,161 --> 00:19:00,160
This is just nib, correct?

409
00:19:00,161 --> 00:19:01,160
That's right.

410
00:19:01,161 --> 00:19:03,160
You haven't, like, added any oil or anything to this?

411
00:19:03,161 --> 00:19:04,460
No, nothing up my sleeve.

412
00:19:04,461 --> 00:19:05,660
That's right.

413
00:19:05,661 --> 00:19:08,380
This melty liquid is just pure cacao nib,

414
00:19:08,381 --> 00:19:11,380
ground up and heated down to a rich liquid

415
00:19:11,381 --> 00:19:13,480
by the friction inside the melanger.

416
00:19:13,481 --> 00:19:14,780
The way this works, basically,

417
00:19:14,781 --> 00:19:16,820
is these are big granite wheels,

418
00:19:16,821 --> 00:19:19,020
and they're rolling on a granite base.

419
00:19:19,021 --> 00:19:21,060
They're in contact at the bottom there,

420
00:19:21,061 --> 00:19:22,600
and we control the amount of pressure

421
00:19:22,601 --> 00:19:24,100
that's being applied to them.

422
00:19:24,101 --> 00:19:26,100
Using stone to grind chocolate

423
00:19:26,101 --> 00:19:28,900
isn't a modern innovation.

424
00:19:28,901 --> 00:19:32,560
The Olmecs and the Toltecs were cultures in Mesoamerica,

425
00:19:32,561 --> 00:19:35,600
and they are believed to be the first culture to cultivate

426
00:19:35,601 --> 00:19:37,640
and to start breeding cacao.

427
00:19:37,641 --> 00:19:40,480
Nobody knows why, but one of the theories is that

428
00:19:40,481 --> 00:19:43,520
there weren't a whole lot of stimulants growing natively,

429
00:19:43,521 --> 00:19:45,180
and so they would have consumed it.

430
00:19:45,181 --> 00:19:46,820
It would have made them feel energized,

431
00:19:46,821 --> 00:19:48,520
like drinking a cup of coffee,

432
00:19:48,521 --> 00:19:52,360
and that some of the substances actually get you high,

433
00:19:52,400 --> 00:19:56,520
and they would have used it as part of their sacred rituals.

434
00:19:56,560 --> 00:19:59,760
So the Olmecs passed cacao onto the Mayans,

435
00:19:59,800 --> 00:20:02,840
and cacao is really important to the Mayans as well.

436
00:20:02,860 --> 00:20:05,440
The name chocolate is derived from a Mayan word,

437
00:20:05,480 --> 00:20:07,680
chocolatel, which literally means bitter water.

438
00:20:07,700 --> 00:20:09,640
This bitter water was a drink

439
00:20:09,680 --> 00:20:11,880
made by mixing ground cacao and water,

440
00:20:11,920 --> 00:20:14,680
and its use didn't end with the Mayans.

441
00:20:14,720 --> 00:20:17,240
The Aztecs used chocolate for a variety of things.

442
00:20:17,241 --> 00:20:20,080
they would give it to people before they were sacrificed

443
00:20:20,180 --> 00:20:23,240
to the gods, and it was supposed to give them courage.

444
00:20:23,340 --> 00:20:25,940
That definitely redefines the phrase

445
00:20:26,040 --> 00:20:27,980
death by chocolate.

446
00:20:28,080 --> 00:20:31,280
There's a really famous story of Montezuma

447
00:20:31,380 --> 00:20:35,400
drinking 50 jars of chocolate at the end

448
00:20:35,500 --> 00:20:38,800
of this big ceremony and banquet,

449
00:20:38,900 --> 00:20:40,800
and then going off to his harem.

450
00:20:40,900 --> 00:20:43,440
I'm not sure if I could put away 50 jars

451
00:20:43,540 --> 00:20:46,700
of Shane's bitter water, but there's only one way

452
00:20:46,701 --> 00:20:49,400
One way to find out.

453
00:20:49,401 --> 00:20:50,600
Is this creamy?

454
00:20:50,601 --> 00:20:51,800
Well, you can take a taste if you like.

455
00:20:51,801 --> 00:20:53,100
I would like to very much.

456
00:20:53,101 --> 00:20:55,100
Yeah, I mean, at this point, completely unsweetened there.

457
00:20:55,101 --> 00:20:57,540
So, you know, it's going to be a little on the bitter side.

458
00:20:57,541 --> 00:20:58,340
Ah, got it.

459
00:20:58,341 --> 00:20:59,240
OK.

460
00:21:08,760 --> 00:21:10,260
Whoa.

461
00:21:16,700 --> 00:21:20,240
Mm-hmm.

462
00:21:20,241 --> 00:21:23,540
Here's the thing, right?

463
00:21:23,541 --> 00:21:25,040
It's not bad.

464
00:21:25,041 --> 00:21:27,540
It's just the richest single bite of food

465
00:21:27,541 --> 00:21:29,040
I may have ever eaten.

466
00:21:29,041 --> 00:21:31,540
I don't want to make it seem at all to you guys at home

467
00:21:31,541 --> 00:21:33,660
like, ugh, it was yucky.

468
00:21:33,661 --> 00:21:38,160
It's chocolate, but the most pure, most intense chocolate

469
00:21:38,161 --> 00:21:40,660
flavor I can possibly think of.

470
00:21:40,661 --> 00:21:44,900
It's like that's like the soul of chocolate.

471
00:21:44,901 --> 00:21:48,440
So we're going to add sugar, and this is cocoa butter

472
00:21:48,540 --> 00:21:49,440
that you've melted?

473
00:21:49,540 --> 00:21:50,540
That's right.

474
00:21:50,640 --> 00:21:54,340
It's about 8% cocoa butter added to the system,

475
00:21:54,440 --> 00:21:56,780
in addition to the naturally occurring cocoa butter

476
00:21:56,880 --> 00:21:57,640
within the beans.

477
00:21:57,740 --> 00:22:00,020
So now the elements that we all love about chocolate,

478
00:22:00,120 --> 00:22:02,620
the creaminess, and the sweetness, we now get to add.

479
00:22:02,720 --> 00:22:04,320
So which do we add first?

480
00:22:04,420 --> 00:22:06,260
So all the cocoa butter can go in now.

481
00:22:06,360 --> 00:22:07,720
We're going to add the sugar kind of gradually.

482
00:22:07,820 --> 00:22:08,860
OK.

483
00:22:08,960 --> 00:22:09,920
So I'm going to go ahead and dump this.

484
00:22:09,921 --> 00:22:13,220
Yes.

485
00:22:13,221 --> 00:22:18,000
So you see now how the texture is, like, really liquidy.

486
00:22:18,001 --> 00:22:20,100
You know, in a second, we're going to start adding sugar,

487
00:22:20,101 --> 00:22:22,640
and that's going to tighten it back up.

488
00:22:22,641 --> 00:22:24,500
And then these sugar crystals are going to refine down

489
00:22:24,501 --> 00:22:26,300
to, like, microscopic size.

490
00:22:26,301 --> 00:22:28,380
So we're going to add this sort of gradually here.

491
00:22:28,381 --> 00:22:30,100
I'm going to put, yeah, absolutely.

492
00:22:30,101 --> 00:22:30,940
Go ahead.

493
00:22:30,941 --> 00:22:31,840
Thanks.

494
00:22:31,841 --> 00:22:32,740
So just slowly?

495
00:22:32,741 --> 00:22:33,940
Yeah, you can go ahead and start pouring

496
00:22:33,941 --> 00:22:36,020
until we feel and hear the machine start to kind of,

497
00:22:36,021 --> 00:22:38,980
like, slow down a little bit.

498
00:22:38,981 --> 00:22:41,080
I can only imagine once we add sugar,

499
00:22:41,081 --> 00:22:45,280
this is going to be absolutely an explosion of flavor.

500
00:22:45,281 --> 00:22:48,160
We're refining and we're conching here.

501
00:22:48,161 --> 00:22:50,800
What is the definition of conching?

502
00:22:50,801 --> 00:22:55,320
As the particles run below the roller stones there,

503
00:22:55,321 --> 00:22:57,260
they're breaking down and they're getting smaller.

504
00:22:57,261 --> 00:22:59,160
Well, if you look at the chocolate under a microscope,

505
00:22:59,161 --> 00:23:01,500
generally, you're going to have a lot of small particles,

506
00:23:01,501 --> 00:23:03,200
but they're going to be all jagged and misshapen.

507
00:23:03,201 --> 00:23:06,140
But conching kind of polishes off those surfaces,

508
00:23:06,141 --> 00:23:08,140
nice creamy mouthfeel that we associate with, like,

509
00:23:08,141 --> 00:23:12,580
really fine chocolate is the result of little tiny spheres,

510
00:23:12,581 --> 00:23:14,740
as opposed to little tiny, jagged little pieces.

511
00:23:14,741 --> 00:23:17,620
In some cases, the particles of chocolate here

512
00:23:17,621 --> 00:23:20,060
can measure nearly 50 times smaller

513
00:23:20,061 --> 00:23:22,280
than a single coffee ground.

514
00:23:22,281 --> 00:23:24,480
At that point, those solid particles

515
00:23:24,481 --> 00:23:26,860
are small enough that we perceive them as just

516
00:23:26,861 --> 00:23:28,700
a creamy, uniform mixture.

517
00:23:31,420 --> 00:23:34,000
Once we get all this loaded in, the next thing we're going to do

518
00:23:34,001 --> 00:23:35,900
is we're going to start kind of messing with the tension

519
00:23:35,901 --> 00:23:36,900
a little bit.

520
00:23:36,901 --> 00:23:38,100
on both sides evenly.

521
00:23:38,101 --> 00:23:39,240
Wait a minute, wait a second.

522
00:23:39,241 --> 00:23:41,400
Let's add some tense violins.

523
00:23:41,401 --> 00:23:44,240
Go.

524
00:23:44,241 --> 00:23:46,140
I feel the tension in the air.

525
00:23:46,141 --> 00:23:48,140
We're going to try to do this nice and evenly.

526
00:23:48,141 --> 00:23:50,240
More violins.

527
00:23:50,241 --> 00:23:51,140
Bring up the strings.

528
00:23:55,160 --> 00:23:56,460
Chocolate.

529
00:23:56,461 --> 00:23:58,560
Just saying the word conjures up images

530
00:23:58,561 --> 00:24:00,860
of some of our favorite treats,

531
00:24:00,861 --> 00:24:03,260
like Reese's chocolate peanut butter cups,

532
00:24:03,261 --> 00:24:05,900
Snickers bars, and M&M's.

533
00:24:05,901 --> 00:24:08,700
While these sweets are among the top five highest selling

534
00:24:08,800 --> 00:24:11,380
chocolate candies in the country,

535
00:24:11,480 --> 00:24:13,480
craft chocolate makers like Sheen

536
00:24:13,580 --> 00:24:15,940
have managed to muscle into the market.

537
00:24:16,040 --> 00:24:20,080
Annual craft chocolate sales are growing more than 20 times

538
00:24:20,180 --> 00:24:23,280
faster than the mainstream competition.

539
00:24:23,380 --> 00:24:26,020
And there may be no single craft chocolate maker

540
00:24:26,120 --> 00:24:28,760
more well known than Jacques Torres.

541
00:24:32,160 --> 00:24:35,500
I have kind of a dream job, making chocolate

542
00:24:35,501 --> 00:24:38,000
There are these trees that you can do with chocolates.

543
00:24:38,001 --> 00:24:41,300
You can sculpt it, you can melt it, you can mold it.

544
00:24:41,301 --> 00:24:45,340
I mean, I just love to work with it.

545
00:24:45,341 --> 00:24:49,540
An accomplished chef, Jacques has built a chocolate empire

546
00:24:49,541 --> 00:24:52,880
on the strength of his name, with seven retail locations

547
00:24:52,881 --> 00:24:56,880
in New York City and a 40,000-square-foot chocolate

548
00:24:56,881 --> 00:24:59,700
factory in Brooklyn that ships to customers

549
00:24:59,701 --> 00:25:01,160
around the country.

550
00:25:01,161 --> 00:25:04,120
With sales approaching $10 million a year,

551
00:25:04,121 --> 00:25:08,820
it's no wonder Jacques' nickname is Mr. Chocolate.

552
00:25:08,821 --> 00:25:14,760
The company uses over 150 tons of chocolate annually,

553
00:25:14,761 --> 00:25:17,160
and all that chocolatey goodness gets combined

554
00:25:17,161 --> 00:25:19,640
with an array of tasty ingredients

555
00:25:19,641 --> 00:25:24,340
to create 300 tons worth of sweets every year.

556
00:25:24,341 --> 00:25:26,080
The number one selling product

557
00:25:26,081 --> 00:25:27,440
going to be the bonbon.

558
00:25:27,441 --> 00:25:31,640
In French, bonbon literally translates to good, good,

559
00:25:31,641 --> 00:25:34,140
and it's no wonder they had to say it twice.

560
00:25:34,141 --> 00:25:36,940
This best-selling confection has two layers.

561
00:25:36,941 --> 00:25:39,880
On the outside is a tempered chocolate shell,

562
00:25:39,881 --> 00:25:43,680
and on the inside is a filling called ganache.

563
00:25:43,681 --> 00:25:47,820
Ganache is a blend of melted chocolate and heavy cream.

564
00:25:47,821 --> 00:25:49,620
To make these decadent nuggets,

565
00:25:49,621 --> 00:25:54,060
Jacques uses a machine that oozes a slow and steady stream

566
00:25:54,061 --> 00:25:55,060
of chocolate.

567
00:25:55,061 --> 00:25:57,200
It's called the enrober,

568
00:25:57,201 --> 00:26:00,500
and its magic begins with ganache filling.

569
00:26:00,501 --> 00:26:03,500
We're going to remove some of the filling from the mixer.

570
00:26:03,600 --> 00:26:06,740
First, 24 pounds of liquid ganache filling

571
00:26:06,840 --> 00:26:11,240
are poured into two plaques and left to set.

572
00:26:11,340 --> 00:26:14,640
When the liquid filling has cooled into solid slabs,

573
00:26:14,740 --> 00:26:20,560
they're cut into 1,600 individual pieces.

574
00:26:20,660 --> 00:26:24,560
And now I'm putting it into this frame,

575
00:26:24,660 --> 00:26:28,000
and I'm going to run that through the rubbing machine.

576
00:26:28,001 --> 00:26:31,840
The solid pieces of ganache filling will run through the

577
00:26:31,940 --> 00:26:36,500
enrober, and as they roll by, the enrober will pour warm

578
00:26:36,600 --> 00:26:38,840
tempered chocolate over them.

579
00:26:38,940 --> 00:26:40,940
That's why we call that an enrobing line.

580
00:26:41,040 --> 00:26:44,240
It enrobe whatever center we put inside.

581
00:26:44,340 --> 00:26:49,060
Then the chocolate go under a fan to push the excess, shake a

582
00:26:49,160 --> 00:26:52,300
little bit, and go into a cooling tunnel that's going to

583
00:26:52,400 --> 00:26:56,300
refrigerate the product.

584
00:26:56,301 --> 00:26:58,860
But before the chocolates enter the cooling tunnel,

585
00:26:59,220 --> 00:27:01,300
there's one important step.

586
00:27:02,540 --> 00:27:06,500
So in between the unrogging machine and the cooling tunnel,

587
00:27:06,860 --> 00:27:07,800
we have this space.

588
00:27:08,100 --> 00:27:10,280
This is the decoration space.

589
00:27:11,700 --> 00:27:13,360
Sometimes we're going to work with colour,

590
00:27:13,960 --> 00:27:17,320
sometimes with texture, and sometimes with design.

591
00:27:18,240 --> 00:27:21,980
For this batch, as the wet chocolates pass the decoration space,

592
00:27:21,981 --> 00:27:24,680
Assistant Production Manager Rachel Barba

593
00:27:24,681 --> 00:27:27,480
lays a transfer sheet over them.

594
00:27:27,481 --> 00:27:32,980
This will transfer the image onto the surface of the bomb box.

595
00:27:32,981 --> 00:27:35,120
Transfer sheets are plastic sheets

596
00:27:35,121 --> 00:27:40,120
printed with images drawn in edible ink.

597
00:27:40,121 --> 00:27:42,140
It's the incredible artistry of the folks

598
00:27:42,141 --> 00:27:44,640
who shaped these sculptures that makes them more

599
00:27:44,641 --> 00:27:46,940
than just a tasty sweet.

600
00:27:46,941 --> 00:27:51,440
When you remove the transfer sheets here,

601
00:27:51,441 --> 00:27:55,940
You get the decoration that was on the transfer sheets

602
00:27:55,941 --> 00:27:56,940
into the chocolates.

603
00:28:00,240 --> 00:28:01,680
Voila.

604
00:28:01,681 --> 00:28:03,560
The bonbons at Jacques Torres

605
00:28:03,561 --> 00:28:06,060
wouldn't be out of place in a museum,

606
00:28:06,061 --> 00:28:08,200
if they weren't so tasty.

607
00:28:08,201 --> 00:28:10,860
And over at Shane, I'm getting a sneak peek

608
00:28:10,861 --> 00:28:13,200
at their own delectable works of art.

609
00:28:13,201 --> 00:28:15,640
This fine, snazzly-dressed gentleman to my left

610
00:28:15,641 --> 00:28:17,360
is Ryan Burley, one of the owners here.

611
00:28:17,361 --> 00:28:19,640
This is called clear toy candy.

612
00:28:19,641 --> 00:28:20,360
Is that right?

613
00:28:20,361 --> 00:28:21,440
Yes.

614
00:28:21,441 --> 00:28:23,980
A tradition that dates to the 18th century,

615
00:28:23,981 --> 00:28:26,520
clear toy candies were gifted to children

616
00:28:26,521 --> 00:28:29,180
during Easter and Christmas.

617
00:28:29,181 --> 00:28:33,320
Shane keeps this tradition alive and only makes these candies

618
00:28:33,321 --> 00:28:34,920
during the holidays.

619
00:28:34,921 --> 00:28:39,560
Cheaply made sugar toys would obviously be the kid's dream.

620
00:28:39,561 --> 00:28:42,420
These days, between January and Easter,

621
00:28:42,421 --> 00:28:47,340
Cadbury's produces 1.5 million holiday cream eggs

622
00:28:47,341 --> 00:28:48,900
every single day.

623
00:28:48,901 --> 00:28:53,880
But imagine unwrapping a holiday treat that's also a toy.

624
00:28:53,881 --> 00:28:56,540
So it's actually a fairly simple process.

625
00:28:56,541 --> 00:28:58,180
It's three ingredients.

626
00:28:58,181 --> 00:29:00,620
It's sugar, water.

627
00:29:00,621 --> 00:29:03,340
And this is glucose, confectioners' glucose.

628
00:29:03,341 --> 00:29:05,940
This stuff's not super easy to work with.

629
00:29:05,941 --> 00:29:07,020
It's thick.

630
00:29:07,021 --> 00:29:07,820
Oh, really?

631
00:29:07,821 --> 00:29:09,580
So we just go right in with our hands.

632
00:29:09,581 --> 00:29:11,800
Oh, man, it's like almost like silly putty.

633
00:29:11,801 --> 00:29:14,660
You're welcome to get into this if you.

634
00:29:14,661 --> 00:29:16,800
I wouldn't mind.

635
00:29:16,801 --> 00:29:18,160
So I put my hand in the water?

636
00:29:18,161 --> 00:29:20,820
Yep, and then just dunk it in there.

637
00:29:20,821 --> 00:29:24,700
Here, you can put it into there just to give a sense of it.

638
00:29:24,701 --> 00:29:25,900
Oh, my gosh.

639
00:29:25,901 --> 00:29:28,200
I have to admit it is such a cool feeling.

640
00:29:28,201 --> 00:29:32,440
Like, it's somewhere between syrup and, like, I don't know,

641
00:29:32,441 --> 00:29:33,940
like wax or something.

642
00:29:33,941 --> 00:29:36,320
I want to recommend to everyone at home,

643
00:29:36,321 --> 00:29:38,320
go get yourself some glucose and play with it.

644
00:29:38,321 --> 00:29:40,540
It is the grooviest thing ever.

645
00:29:40,541 --> 00:29:42,760
This is the time for us to wash our hands.

646
00:29:42,761 --> 00:29:44,920
Ever wonder how lollipops are made?

647
00:29:44,921 --> 00:29:47,360
It's just like this, except instead

648
00:29:47,361 --> 00:29:50,900
Instead of using glucose, it's corn syrup that gets boiled

649
00:29:50,901 --> 00:29:52,360
with the sugar and water.

650
00:29:52,361 --> 00:29:55,040
And then the sweet sap gets molded.

651
00:29:55,041 --> 00:30:00,040
But instead of a lollipop, we're using toy candy molds.

652
00:30:00,041 --> 00:30:01,540
Oh, wow.

653
00:30:01,541 --> 00:30:03,180
Oh, I love that smell.

654
00:30:03,181 --> 00:30:04,580
It smells like cotton candy.

655
00:30:04,581 --> 00:30:07,920
There's this, oh, it's like a toasted sugar smell.

656
00:30:07,921 --> 00:30:08,920
Yeah, it is.

657
00:30:08,921 --> 00:30:10,680
It's like a toasted marshmallow.

658
00:30:10,681 --> 00:30:12,980
Once each mold is carefully filled,

659
00:30:12,981 --> 00:30:14,960
all it takes is a few minutes rest

660
00:30:14,961 --> 00:30:17,960
before we can start cracking these babies open.

661
00:30:17,961 --> 00:30:19,960
Wow.

662
00:30:19,961 --> 00:30:20,960
Wow, that's Anta.

663
00:30:20,961 --> 00:30:22,960
Look at that one.

664
00:30:22,961 --> 00:30:23,960
Nice job.

665
00:30:23,961 --> 00:30:27,960
Look at the detail on his face and the hands and the robe.

666
00:30:27,961 --> 00:30:28,960
That's a beauty.

667
00:30:28,961 --> 00:30:30,960
And how old are these bolts?

668
00:30:30,961 --> 00:30:32,960
They're probably 125 years old.

669
00:30:38,960 --> 00:30:39,960
Oh, man.

670
00:30:39,961 --> 00:30:41,960
Come on.

671
00:30:41,961 --> 00:30:43,960
Come on, man.

672
00:30:43,961 --> 00:30:46,120
Man, that is gorgeous.

673
00:30:46,121 --> 00:30:46,960
I am blown away.

674
00:30:46,961 --> 00:30:49,000
I am legitimately blown away.

675
00:30:49,001 --> 00:30:50,560
Oh, there's William Penn.

676
00:30:50,561 --> 00:30:52,000
And for those of you who don't know even the name

677
00:30:52,001 --> 00:30:55,240
of the state, Pennsylvania means Penn's Woods.

678
00:30:55,241 --> 00:30:56,980
We've lost his head.

679
00:30:56,981 --> 00:30:58,140
May I respectfully hear?

680
00:30:58,141 --> 00:30:59,880
Can I have William Penn's head?

681
00:30:59,881 --> 00:31:00,581
May I try it?

682
00:31:00,582 --> 00:31:01,880
Oh, please.

683
00:31:01,881 --> 00:31:05,080
And I feel it's only appropriate if I'm

684
00:31:05,081 --> 00:31:07,680
going to be in Philadelphia to have William Penn.

685
00:31:07,681 --> 00:31:08,381
Mmm.

686
00:31:12,180 --> 00:31:14,140
It's odd, because even though it's not caramel,

687
00:31:14,141 --> 00:31:15,640
it has that butteriness to it.

688
00:31:15,641 --> 00:31:16,640
Yeah.

689
00:31:16,641 --> 00:31:17,640
Mmm.

690
00:31:20,940 --> 00:31:24,020
That is delicious.

691
00:31:24,021 --> 00:31:26,020
All right, let me let this man get back to his work.

692
00:31:26,021 --> 00:31:28,020
Let's go and catch up with Kevin, and let's

693
00:31:28,021 --> 00:31:30,020
continue our chocolate odyssey.

694
00:31:32,580 --> 00:31:36,880
Who hasn't Jacques Torres created a special dessert for?

695
00:31:36,881 --> 00:31:42,300
Is it Ronald Reagan, me, Adam Richman, the Pope, or Hugh Hefner?

696
00:31:46,020 --> 00:31:49,800
To date, Jacques Torres, though he has met Adam Richman,

697
00:31:49,801 --> 00:31:53,360
has not created a specialized dessert for Adam Richman.

698
00:31:53,361 --> 00:31:56,620
Adam Richman is waiting patiently and is certainly not

699
00:31:56,621 --> 00:31:58,940
taking that too personally.

700
00:31:58,941 --> 00:32:02,420
This batch should be done at this point.

701
00:32:02,421 --> 00:32:05,260
I'm looking at a batch of fresh chocolate

702
00:32:05,261 --> 00:32:08,140
that's been combined with sugar and cocoa butter

703
00:32:08,141 --> 00:32:10,100
into this luscious concoction.

704
00:32:10,101 --> 00:32:13,060
This looks and smells like the chocolate

705
00:32:13,061 --> 00:32:14,800
I've been dreaming about.

706
00:32:14,801 --> 00:32:16,940
We can go ahead and take a taste if you'd like.

707
00:32:16,941 --> 00:32:17,780
I would like.

708
00:32:17,781 --> 00:32:19,020
Get you a little smear here.

709
00:32:19,021 --> 00:32:21,260
So this is our house dark chocolate blend.

710
00:32:21,261 --> 00:32:23,260
All righty.

711
00:32:23,261 --> 00:32:24,580
Let's give this a little taste.

712
00:32:24,581 --> 00:32:25,420
Mmm.

713
00:32:28,180 --> 00:32:29,020
Mmm.

714
00:32:33,320 --> 00:32:35,020
This is the droid I've been looking for.

715
00:32:36,020 --> 00:32:38,500
This is, it's like the essence of chocolate.

716
00:32:38,501 --> 00:32:40,300
You know, it's so weird.

717
00:32:40,301 --> 00:32:42,460
You put it in your mouth and you're like,

718
00:32:42,461 --> 00:32:44,340
I guess your mind goes to any time

719
00:32:44,341 --> 00:32:46,140
you've had that liquid chocolate consistency.

720
00:32:46,141 --> 00:32:49,100
So you think of hot fudge or the thing you put in your milk

721
00:32:49,101 --> 00:32:50,840
to make chocolate milk.

722
00:32:50,841 --> 00:32:53,100
It's kind of remarkable because I only associate

723
00:32:53,101 --> 00:32:55,100
with milk chocolate,

724
00:32:55,101 --> 00:32:58,100
and yet here it is with this dark chocolate blend.

725
00:32:58,101 --> 00:33:01,100
The difference between milk and dark chocolate

726
00:33:01,101 --> 00:33:03,780
depends partly on who's making it.

727
00:33:03,781 --> 00:33:05,780
Dark chocolate typically contains

728
00:33:05,781 --> 00:33:07,780
at least 30% cocoa solids,

729
00:33:07,781 --> 00:33:11,480
plus cocoa butter, sugar, and often vanilla.

730
00:33:11,481 --> 00:33:14,380
Cocoa solids are the tiny solid particles

731
00:33:14,381 --> 00:33:17,120
in ground-up liquid chocolate.

732
00:33:17,121 --> 00:33:20,120
Milk chocolate only has a third of the solids

733
00:33:20,121 --> 00:33:21,800
found in dark chocolate.

734
00:33:21,801 --> 00:33:26,000
A craft or artisan milk chocolate often contains much more cocoa,

735
00:33:26,240 --> 00:33:29,640
35 to 55%, which makes it more expensive,

736
00:33:29,920 --> 00:33:31,340
but also more intensely flavored.

737
00:33:32,280 --> 00:33:35,040
Expensive chocolate has roots in history.

738
00:33:35,600 --> 00:33:37,700
As the 19th century is advancing,

739
00:33:38,020 --> 00:33:41,900
a number of people get into this business of making bar chocolate,

740
00:33:41,901 --> 00:33:48,980
and it's an expensive, high-class kind of snack for the elites of Europe.

741
00:33:48,981 --> 00:33:53,360
Europeans mainly drank chocolate refined with sugar

742
00:33:53,361 --> 00:33:54,960
until the Industrial Revolution.

743
00:33:55,840 --> 00:33:57,600
And by the beginning of the 20th century,

744
00:33:58,080 --> 00:33:59,900
Milton Hershey sought to find a way

745
00:33:59,901 --> 00:34:02,520
to bring this elite treat to the masses.

746
00:34:03,060 --> 00:34:06,440
By 1900, he spends some time trying to figure out,

747
00:34:06,500 --> 00:34:08,820
okay, how do I make milk chocolate?

748
00:34:09,180 --> 00:34:11,940
He wants to keep the stuff cheap and available.

749
00:34:13,120 --> 00:34:15,180
So how do you make chocolate cheap?

750
00:34:15,280 --> 00:34:16,000
Chocolate's expensive.

751
00:34:16,100 --> 00:34:17,620
No matter how you cut it, chocolate's expensive.

752
00:34:17,621 --> 00:34:18,980
You have to import it.

753
00:34:19,060 --> 00:34:20,480
You have to grind it.

754
00:34:21,260 --> 00:34:24,440
What's cheap is sugar and milk.

755
00:34:25,700 --> 00:34:27,620
He's in the middle of dairy country in Pennsylvania.

756
00:34:28,260 --> 00:34:31,580
And so he founds this town called Hershey, Pennsylvania,

757
00:34:31,760 --> 00:34:35,200
builds a giant factory, and starts producing Hershey's bars.

758
00:34:37,780 --> 00:34:40,500
Milton Hershey's genius is that he figured out

759
00:34:40,501 --> 00:34:42,040
how to make chocolate affordable

760
00:34:42,041 --> 00:34:45,320
so that everyone with a nickel in their pocket

761
00:34:45,321 --> 00:34:47,000
could buy a bar of chocolate.

762
00:34:47,001 --> 00:34:49,500
And what this does is it took chocolate

763
00:34:49,501 --> 00:34:52,500
from being an artisan, handmade product

764
00:34:52,501 --> 00:34:55,340
into a truly industrial product

765
00:34:55,341 --> 00:34:58,340
that everybody in the world could afford to purchase.

766
00:34:58,341 --> 00:35:00,340
You know, this idea of, like, Hershey's chocolate

767
00:35:00,341 --> 00:35:02,840
is so synonymous with American culture, you know?

768
00:35:02,841 --> 00:35:04,760
These snacks that we really think of

769
00:35:04,761 --> 00:35:06,960
when we think of chocolate broadly

770
00:35:06,961 --> 00:35:09,960
are these things that Hershey's brought to the American market.

771
00:35:09,961 --> 00:35:11,960
Over a century later,

772
00:35:11,961 --> 00:35:14,460
Hershey's business model has paid off.

773
00:35:14,461 --> 00:35:19,100
In 2018, they alone were responsible for 43%

774
00:35:19,101 --> 00:35:22,300
of the total U.S. chocolate market.

775
00:35:22,301 --> 00:35:25,100
And they produce affordable chocolate candies

776
00:35:25,101 --> 00:35:27,300
at a staggering rate.

777
00:35:27,301 --> 00:35:32,020
70 million Hershey Kisses are made every single day.

778
00:35:32,021 --> 00:35:36,020
And all of it happens in the great state of Pennsylvania,

779
00:35:36,021 --> 00:35:38,220
not far from where I've just had a taste

780
00:35:38,221 --> 00:35:40,460
of Shane's magical chocolate.

781
00:35:40,461 --> 00:35:41,620
Mm.

782
00:35:41,621 --> 00:35:43,620
But as delicious as this is,

